WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:01.910
Film Courage: What is churn rate?

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:07.369
Houston Howard: Churn rate is how soon quickly
people get sick of something.

00:00:07.369 --> 00:00:13.800
So a kid will play with a toy for 5 minutes
and then get sick of it and move to something

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.850
else.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:22.320
That’s churn rate and the faster the churn
rate the worse it is because people get sick

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.090
of your stuff in a much quicker rate.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:31.769
You’re flipping through the channels you
land on a show, how long are you going to

00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:33.930
give that before you flip to the next channel?

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.200
On the radio, how long are you going to give
a song before you flip to the next song?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:46.280
Churn rates typically in a competitive environment
are very high because there are so many options

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.280
now.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.170
I’m just like bouncing from thing to thing
to thing to thing.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:01:00.320
Back in the 1950’s when there was just radio,
then people sit and listen to one radio station

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.970
for hours and just listen to the radio dramas
and news and they wouldn’t flip to anything

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:09.090
because there was only one thing that they
could do now with all the stuff that we have

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.790
to do and all of the entertainment options,
the oversaturated tsunami of entertainment

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.670
that is around us all the time.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.280
Churn rates are very high and that is bad
for creators.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.979
We now have to think of how we create.

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:30.220
How do we create a brand that lowers the churn
rate of people?

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:36.370
How do we keep them engaged for longer and
in more meaningful ways into our brand, into

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:37.920
our stories.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.530
That’s something very important because
the longer you can engage the audience and

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.890
the longer the valuable engagement takes place,
the more chance you have to then convert them

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:54.380
to that loyal audience member or ultimately
to what I call a brand evangelist, somebody

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.360
who is not just watching your stuff but actually
helps market your stuff.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.350
“Hey, did you see the new Game of Thrones
this week?”

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.369
“Hey you should check out this show You
should go watch this movie because it’s

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:04.280
awesome.”

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:11.170
If you can convert them into that, that’s
the sweet spot because then you get people

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.500
1) loyal to your brand and 2) that are so
loyal that they’re marketing your brand

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.380
you start to solve some of that P and A [Prints
and Advertising] issue we talked about earlier

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:24.380
of having to rely on so much P and A because
you’ve activated your fan base to do something

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.380
valuable.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.599
But you’re never going to activate a fanbase
if they only engage with your stories 10 seconds

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:31.709
at a time.

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:39.220
So now we have to slow that down, get them
engaged and have that engagement be valuable.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.760
And if you can do that, then all of the sudden
you’ve separated yourself in the marketplace

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.620
and solved a major problem in the industry
which is these high churn rate issues.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.010
Film Courage: So does this mean we keep our
content shorter or give [the audience] breaks?

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.920
Houston Howard: Well, it depends.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.319
It depends on your content.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:03.580
But the thing that people love most is story.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:09.230
We live in an entertainment culture and people
love story more than anything else.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:16.390
Whereas attention spans may be seemingly low,
they’re no longer low if you can engage

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:17.450
them with story.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.790
If you have a great story, they’ll watch
it for 10 hours in a row.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:21.790
And that’s fine.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:28.810
So a 10-hour binge watch is a very low churn
rate, they’re watching for 10 hours.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.120
They are not turning off.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.870
This is where technique and craft come into
play.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.980
If you can understand how to hook them, hook
them fast, give them new and better things

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.239
are going through the story, then all of the
sudden you’re starting to lower that churn

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:50.600
rate but at the same time it’s how do we
create multiple engagements that if you’re

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:56.690
operating in just network television you have
one 1-hour engagement every week and that’s

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.690
it.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:05.300
And so now between your one one-hour engagements
you have a week in between, right?

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.890
If we can start filling that week with some
other things.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:13.360
As a companion web series like they do on
AMC.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.040
Maybe a digital comic that gives them more
valuable parts of the story that bridges the

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.799
gap between episodes.

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:25.990
What you do every single time is you have
your audience engage with valuable content,

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:32.840
valuable story, then slowly you are going
to start to lower their churn rate until you

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.040
get them to the point that they will watch
your stuff for 10 hours.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.560
Typically they won’t do that at the beginning.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.061
It’s a process to getting them to that point
to where they will say “Okay, I’ll watch

00:04:42.061 --> 00:04:51.340
your stuff” “I’ll watch 60 hours of
your content” through a binge watch you’ve

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:56.490
spent the time and made the investment in
the “me” as an audience member to a point

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.930
to where I’m willing to give this investment
into your brand.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:07.440
And the only way to get to that point is to
have as much and as many valuable engagements

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.930
as humanly possible and the thing that they
value most is story.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.190
So it’s not just marketing and promotions.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:17.729
That’s a big transmedia misconception and
it’s all advertising and marketing.

00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:21.680
It’s really story…the best is story-based
stuff.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.560
How do I create story-based engagement?

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:36.200
So at SXSW the show Bates Motel, they created
a replica of the Bates Motel that you could

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.449
actually rent out a room and stay in while
you were in SXSW.

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:42.040
That’s cool marketing!

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:47.449
But it’s not transmedia because it didn’t
give you more of the story because what happens

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:51.240
is when it’s time to go to the festival,
they would open the door and leave the room

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.740
and come back and they had very limited time
in the room.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:01.710
But if some way the took a picture off of
the wall to discover that Norman [Bates] had

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:04.050
written a message behind that picture.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:08.100
If they were to take the Gideon’s Bible
out of the desk and it’s not a Gideon’s

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.100
Bible at all.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.670
And maybe there is an object in there.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:15.280
Maybe it’s a journal written by Norman that
gives more insight into his character and

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.470
some insight into some story lines.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:21.430
There are creaky floorboard that you can pop
up and stuff under there.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:25.979
You flip over the mattress and there is stuff
in the mattress, maybe stuff in the mattress

00:06:25.979 --> 00:06:27.759
you have to rip open and find.

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:35.700
If you can extend the story in such a way
that the hotel room becomes part of the story…now…now

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:42.449
what has happened is you’ve created longer
points of engagement for that fan base.

00:06:42.449 --> 00:06:46.630
And the fans that are in the room maybe don’t
want to go outside because they whole room

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.360
is part of the story that they love.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.350
Presumably they don’t stay in the Bates
Motel unless they watch that Bates Motel show

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:56.660
or even if they do, maybe it’s a mechanism
to get them to watch the show.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.540
But you are going to have a better chance
to get them to watch the show as you start

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.660
to seed the story and extend the story in
a really interesting way.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.610
Maybe you never watched the show before but
based on this experience that you have all

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:13.940
these weird story clues and interactions that
you discover in this room.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:21.270
Now you have an even greater chance of checking
it out far beyond just a marketing experience.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:32.000
It’s how do we create lots of really valuable
story based engagements for the fan base?

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.550
And every single one of those engagements
creates a lower churn rate.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:43.860
So maybe the churn rate is very high for somebody…I’m
ADD, I’m distracted, I like to do a 100

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.990
things, I don’t like watching long-form
TV (I just like watching web stuff, Youtube,

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:49.039
all that stuff).

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:56.229
If you can just slowly start giving story-based
engagement to that audience member and then

00:07:56.229 --> 00:07:59.740
you start to lower the churn rate, lower the
churn rate, lower the churn rate, lower the

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:03.190
churn rate until eventually you can get them
to the point of saying “I’ll watch your

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:08.470
stuff for 10 hours in a sitting” because
you’ve invested into “me” so much all

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:09.470
ready.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:10.470
Does it makes sense?

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:11.470
Film Courage: It does!

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:11.473
Houston Howard: Great!

